## 2. wrangler.jsonc — Durable Objects + Migrations + Assets

**Sources:**
- DO bindings + migrations (legacy `new_sqlite_classes`): https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/ (2026-06-19)
- New `exports` declaration (recommended for new projects): https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/get-started/ (2026-07-15)
- Static Assets binding (`directory`, `binding`, `run_worker_first`, `not_found_handling`): https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/binding/ (2026-04-23)

### Durable Object declaration — two current shapes

As of mid-2026 the docs show **two coexisting** shapes. The newer `exports`-based shape (get-started page, 2026-07-15) is recommended for new projects; the `migrations`/`new_sqlite_classes` shape (still shown on the WebSockets best-practices page, 2026-06-19) is labeled "legacy" by the get-started page but is still the documented pattern for class rename/delete lifecycle management. Both are valid today; I have verified both verbatim against the live docs.

**Newer `exports` shape (recommended for new DO classes):**
```jsonc
{
  "exports": {
    "GameRoom": {
      "type": "durable-object",
      "storage": "sqlite"
    }
  }
}
```

**Legacy `migrations` shape (still documented, required for class-rename migrations):**
```jsonc
{
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "GAME_ROOM", "class_name": "GameRoom" }
    ]
  },
  "migrations": [
    {
      "tag": "v1",
      "new_sqlite_classes": ["GameRoom"]
    }
  ]
}
```

> ⚠️ Flag: Cloudflare is mid-migration between these two declarations. The get-started page (2026-07-15) points readers to the `exports` shape and labels `migrations` as legacy; the WebSockets best-practices page (2026-06-19, one month older) still uses `migrations`/`new_sqlite_classes`. Until the WebSockets page is updated, either is accepted by Wrangler. For a brand-new racing game in 2026, prefer the `exports` shape; keep the `migrations` array only when you need to rename or delete classes across deploys.

### Full wrangler.jsonc for a Hono + DO + static SPA

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "./node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "fallguys-racing",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-24",
  // nodejs_compat is needed only if you import Node built-ins.
  // Hono itself does not require it.
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  "assets": {
    "directory": "./public/",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    // SPA fallback: 404s serve index.html so client-side routing works.
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",
    // Run the Worker (Hono) for API/WS routes; serve assets directly otherwise.
    "run_worker_first": ["/api/*", "/ws/*", "!/api/docs/*"]
  },

  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "GAME_ROOM", "class_name": "GameRoom" }
    ]
  },

  "migrations": [
    {
      "tag": "v1",
      "new_sqlite_classes": ["GameRoom"]
    }
  ],

  "routes": [
    {
      "pattern": "game.example.com",
      "custom_domain": true
    }
  ]
}
```

### Free-plan Durable Objects (SQLite-backed)

> ⚠️ **Could not fully verify from primary docs in this session.** The Durable Objects pricing page (`/durable-objects/platform/pricing/`) and the Workers free-plan page repeatedly exceeded the retrieval output limit, so I could not quote the exact free-plan quota verbatim. What I *can* verify: the current docs use **SQLite-backed DOs** (`new_sqlite_classes` / `storage: "sqlite"`) as the standard, GA-backed DO type, and Cloudflare's published guidance directs all new DOs to SQLite storage. SQLite-backed DOs are the condition under which DOs are usable on the Free plan; the legacy KV-backed DOs required a paid plan. Before relying on this for production, confirm the current free-plan allocation (requests, duration GB-s, storage) at https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing/.

### Assets binding behavior (which requests hit the Worker vs assets)
- `run_worker_first: false` (default): requests matching a static asset are served from the asset store; the Worker runs only for non-asset paths (and for `not_found_handling` evaluation).
- `run_worker_first: true` (or an array of glob patterns): the Worker runs unconditionally (or for matching patterns); the Worker can call `env.ASSETS.fetch(request)` to fetch assets programmatically.
- `not_found_handling: "single-page-application"` makes unmatched asset requests return `index.html` — correct for an SPA game client.
- `binding: "ASSETS"` exposes a `Fetcher` at `env.ASSETS`; call `env.ASSETS.fetch(new Request("https://assets.local/path"))` (hostname is ignored; only the path matters).

### Node compat
`"compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"]` is required only if your code imports Node built-ins (e.g. `node:crypto`, `node:buffer`). Hono itself does **not** require it.

